			<tr>
				<td colspan="2"><span class="postbody"><a href="http://www.STURMBOT.COM" target="_blank">www.STURMBOT.COM</a> DOMAIN NAME THEFT
<br />

<br />
Soldiers,
<br />

<br />
As many of you have already discovered, the <a href="http://www.sturmbot.com" target="_blank">www.sturmbot.com</a> domain name no longer points to the actual Sturmbot website.  As we host the Sturmbot site, you also are aware of its current address <a href="http://sturmbot.houstondod.com." target="_blank">http://sturmbot.houstondod.com.</a>  But, many of you are left wondering what happened to cause the <a href="http://www.sturmbot.com" target="_blank">www.sturmbot.com</a> domain to be redirected.  I'm here to present the facts of this situation to allow the gaming community a better idea of the types of people we have in our midst.  I'll assure you that this domain redirection was no accident.  In working with legal authorities in two countries now (United States and Australia), we are working towards criminal prosecution of this matter.  The facts presented below can be proved with concrete evidence.  As I understand this to be a very serious matter, I would be foolish to post facts that are mere speculation or even "gut instincts".  It's been a while since I abandoned my instincts in favor of hard legal evidence.  I think it is incredibly unfortunate for this to have pointed towards one individual's illegal actions and very twisted identity theft.  Please, read on and consider the facts as presented.
<br />

<br />
Here are the facts of this distrubing situation.  I'll try to follow a somewhat orderly timeline of events.
<br />

<br />
1) About a month ago, we received email asking us why <a href="http://www.sturmbot.com" target="_blank">www.sturmbot.com</a> was being redirected to Shrikebot's message forum address of <a href="http://pub122.ezboard.com/bshrikebot." target="_blank">http://pub122.ezboard.com/bshrikebot.</a>  As this was a surprise to myself (as Sturmbot's web hosting administrator) as well as Jowo (Sturmbot creator and team leader), we had no immediate explanations and passed it off as a "mistake".  We told our readers we would look into the error and make corrections.
<br />

<br />
2) In making contact with the Shrikebot team, we were told that none of them had any idea what had happened.  No one had a clue about how or why the Sturmbot.com address was now being pointed to their forum.
<br />

<br />
3) After being told this by the Shrikebot team, we were faced with a very peculiar situation.  The situation was peculiar because the person who registered the Sturmbot.com domain name had passed away back in October of 2002 in an unfortunate accident.  Phuzzy, or Andrew Power, the sturmbot.com legal owner was killed (as most of you know by now).  He was the only person listed on the sturmbot.com domain registry.  Thus, that means he would have been the only person legally able to make changes to his site registry.  Thus, how could his domain be changed several months after his death?  A strange situation...or was it?
<br />

<br />
4) Upon further and closer examination, we found enom.com to be the registrar of the Sturmbot.com domain name.  Thus, we began our closer investigation at the source of registry.  enom.com still listed Andrew Power as the rightful and legal owner of sturmbot.com.  Everything appeared normal and we were faced with passing this off as "one big mistake".  It would seem that there might have been an honest mistake after all.  Everything appeared as if this was a legal transfer.  We began trying to figure out how we could correct the domain transfer..and restore proper operation to the <a href="http://www.sturmbot.com" target="_blank">www.sturmbot.com</a> domain.  However, we knew we could only legally make changes if the changes were ordered by Andrew Power himself, as again, he was the ONLY legal contact in the domain's registry.  Thus, we figured it was just a "big loss".  We had no way to have Andrew make the changes.  He had passed away and obviously couldn't.  We just about gave up and decided to wait until this coming June and simply renew the registration under another name, Jowo.  Until then, the name would be pointing at Shrikebot...and they would be the recipient of the web hits and traffic that sturmbot.com generated...until June.
<br />

<br />
5) Enter some strange behavior that started us thinking this was foulplay.  A week or two after this alleged "accident", anonymous guests began posting strange things in our message forums about someone or something named "womble".  These messages were peculiar because they often started new threads with things like "does womble scare you" and things of that nature.  Obviously implying some sort of mild "threat" or "taunt".  Well, it didn't take a genius to discover there was currently a person named "Womble" on the Shrikebot team.  Thus, Hulk and a couple of Sturmbot people became suspicious that the Shrikebot team may have not been telling us the complete truth when we first approached them about the domain problem.  It was as if someone was "boasting" of some type of accomplishment through their largely irrelevant "mildly threatening" posts in our forum.  We had to approach this with new suspicion.  We decided to take initiative and approach this as a criminal investigation.
<br />

<br />
6) Calling on my own experience in dealing with security breaches (through my profession) of computer systems and/or network systems, it was time to dig a bit deeper.  A revisit of enom.com was in order.  The only way things could have changed was for Andrew Power, or someone using his identity to order them.  Thus, it was time to search for people who might have gained access to his email account listed on this domain.  While it was very difficult for a hacker to actually hack into his personal email account and send/receive from it...it was not that difficult to set up a phony email forwarding account.  Thus, email from Andrew Power had to be redirected to a 3rd party email address in order to "create the illusion" of Andrew's email being Andrew's.
<br />

<br />
7) In visiting enom.com again, I discovered Andrew Power's email was indeed being forwarded to a 3rd party address.  The address was <a href="mailto:smb@dcemail.com">smb@dcemail.com</a>.  An email address that no one had really heard of.  Who was this <a href="mailto:smb@dcemail.com">smb@dcemail.com</a> email address?  Who had set up forwarding for Andrew's email account?  New questions remained.  They were soon answered.
<br />

<br />
<img src="images/smiles/icon_cool.gif" alt="icon_cool.gif" border="0" /> As I have administrative access to the MySQL database that powers the Sturmbot website (forums, downloads), I decided to do a simple search of the existing member database to see if I could match this strange email address.  I mean, suspicion tells me that if this was indeed criminal activity, the likelihood of this person being in our database might be very probable.  Guess what?  My suspicion was correct.  The <a href="mailto:smb@dcemail.com">smb@dcemail.com</a> email address belonged to a member going by the name of Sgt. Cunt0x.  Thus, we had a new name entered into this picture.  We had proof at this point that the Sgt. Cunt0x identity was receiving Andrew Power's email.  Both were operating with this anonymous email address, <a href="mailto:smb@dcemail.com">smb@dcemail.com</a>.  But, who the heck is Sgt. Cunt0x?  What, if any, are his ties to Shrikebot?  There were still pieces that could not be proven with a degree of legal certainty.  It was all pure instinct and suspicion.  That as you all know, will not hold up in a criminal proceeding.  We had more digging to do.
<br />

<br />
9) Enter more peculiar behavior.  I took it upon myself to sign up as a member of Shrikebot's message forums.  You see, before all this started, I didn't even know there were bots available for Tour of Duty.  I was interested in two things.  One, to actually download, use, and learn about Shrikebot as a product of gaming.  Two, to create a presence on the Shrikebot forums to do some sniffing around.  Well, after posting a couple of generic messages or so, I got an unusually hostile response from a member of their forums, a person called "Womble".  Hmmm, I thought...why would a person immediately be hostile to me?  I had never spoken to anyone named Womble before.  I had no history with the Shrikebot team.  What was the basis for the unusual hostility?  I immediately was reminded of the "mild threats" in the Sturmbot forums about or relating to a "Womble".  It would appear that my bait in the Shrikebot forums caught the fish I was looking for.  I mean, it was very odd for this person to have been immediately hostile with me...unless they knew I was associated with Sturmbot or Houston DoD.  Agreed?  Immediately I suspected this Womble had to have at least one other alias.  Thus...we narrowed our Shrikebot suspects to this Womble person.  He seemed quite proud of himself for some reason.  The idiotic sturmbot forum posting proved something was up.  All fools leave clues.  Remember that.
<br />

<br />
10) Now...the thing remaining was to tie Womble to the email address used by the domain hijacker, <a href="mailto:smb@dcemail.com">smb@dcemail.com</a>.  That was one thing we still could not do.  We had tons of suspicion...but had no real proof Womble was the same person that stole the sturmbot.com domain name.  I turned all this information over to legal authorities in two countries, the US and Australia (as I had captured IP addresses from Sgt. Cunt0x/smb@dcemail.com in our MySQL database).  In addition, we submitted this small pile of information to enom.com's security team, dcemail.com's fraud/abuse team, and one other Internet domain security group for further investigation.  We seemed to be at a dead end.  Our investigation seemed to have run its course.  All we could legally prove was that there were several roads leading to Womble, but we couldn't attach this name to the domain hijacker to the degree necessary for criminal prosecution.  However, every fool leaves clues.  And yes...a week or so later...a very big discovery takes place.
<br />

<br />
11) Our group discovered a posting or two on other website resources that belonged to a person called Womble.  Guess what?  This Womble also used the email address <a href="mailto:smb@dcemail.com">smb@dcemail.com</a>!!!!  For your viewing pleasure, here is the first post, from a Gameguru website...
<br />

<br />
<a href="http://www.ggmania.com/full.php3?show=5506" target="_blank">http://www.ggmania.com/full.php3?show=5506</a>
<br />

<br />
Scroll down a little, Womble's post is from Feb 10, 2003.  And if that wasn't enough...and well...it should be...there is another posting on an Australian penpals forum.  This one, from March 26, 2003.  He used the name "Andy Power" and again, the <a href="mailto:smb@dcemail.com">smb@dcemail.com</a> address.
<br />

<br />
<a href="http://penpalsnow.com/browse/agecountry/AU2330.html" target="_blank">http://penpalsnow.com/browse/agecountry/AU2330.html</a>
<br />

<br />
For the penpal link, you need to click a few pages in to see the "Andy Power" posting.  It would appear our little criminal Womble needs some loving.
<br />

<br />
12) Where does this leave us now?  Working with Australian legal authorities in pinning this Womble/Sgt.Cunt0x character to a human being and a real computer so we can prosecute for counts of identity theft, domain theft, international fraud, and criminal mischief.  We are also working with United States authorities as well as the security teams of enom.com, and two general Internet authorities.  We will get our man.  That's a given.  It just takes some time to do it properly.
<br />

<br />
So...through all this...we can sum this entire case up like this...
<br />

<br />
- This domain redirection was not an accident.  The Shrikebot team (Womble), has stolen Sturmbot.com's web name.  The reasons?  Obvious website traffic gains and recognition.
<br />

<br />
- This Womble person has stooped as low as impersonating the deceased in committing his crimes.
<br />

<br />
- Identity theft, domain hijacking, and other illegal acts will not and should not go UNCHECKED.  Criminals will pay heavily for playing their games.  
<br />

<br />

<br />
There are two extremely important facts that I'll leave you with...
<br />

<br />
1) The Sturmbot.com domain name will once again belong to its rightful ownership when this investigation is complete.  If the Shrikebot team is intelligent, they'll do it before they are forced by legal authority.
<br />

<br />
2) Irrespective of when/if the Sturmbot.com domain name is returned, we will STILL proceed with the criminal investigation and we will CONTINUE prosecution of the guilty party (parties).  There is no escape for the fool who thought he would be bigtime and play games with those who can truly take care of his kiddie behavior.
<br />

<br />
My friends, this is a serious matter of grave consequences.  I highly encourage all of you reading this and caring about Sturmbot, Phuzzy's memory, Jowo, and the rest of the Sturmbot team to take action against Shrikebot's team member Womble.  Send him email.  Tell him what a loser he is and how horrible these unconscionable acts have been.  Join us in the fight against this illegal, immoral, and downright stupid kiddie game.
<br />

<br />
We are taking this very seriously.  We hope you will to.
<br />

<br />
Thank you for reading this unfortunate story,<br />_________________<br />Hulk Bogan
